<!-- ================================================================ -->
<!-- 3. STATE AND TRAJECTORY FRAMEWORK ≈ 900-1100 words.              -->
<!-- ================================================================ -->

<section xml:id="sec-framework">
  <title>State and Trajectory Framework</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The framework models a response first as a complete card-to-zone
      configuration and then as a marked, timestamped trajectory through the
      configuration space.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-configurations">
    <title>Configurations and State Space</title>

    <p>
      For one item, let <m>\cardset=\{1,\ldots,P\}</m> index the cards and let
      <m>\zoneset=\{0,1,\ldots,R\}</m> index the tray and destination slots.  A
      configuration is a function
      <md xml:id="eq-configuration">
        s:\cardset\rightarrow\zoneset,
      </md>
      where <m>s(j)</m> is the zone occupied by card <m>j</m>.  The initial
      configuration is <m>s_0=(0,\ldots,0)</m>, and the key configuration
      <m>s^\star</m> is determined by the authored item source.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same configuration can be represented as a column-stochastic
      zero-one membership matrix
      <md xml:id="eq-membership-space">
        X(s)\in\Xspace
        =
        \left\{
          X\in\{0,1\}^{(R+1)\times P}:
          \mathbf{1}_{R+1}^{\mathsf T}X
          =
          \mathbf{1}_{P}^{\mathsf T}
        \right\}.
      </md>
      Column <m>j</m> contains a single one in row <m>s(j)</m>.  The function,
      vector, and matrix forms encode the same state; the vector form is
      compact, while the matrix form makes validity, occupancy, and agreement
      calculations explicit.
    </p>

    <p>
      The natural distance between configurations, <m>s_1</m> and <m>s_2</m>, is the number of cards
      occupying different zones:
      <md xml:id="eq-state-distance">
        d_{\cardset}(s_1,s_2)
        =
        \#\{j\in\cardset:s_1(j)\neq s_2(j)\}.
      </md>
      In matrix form,
      <md xml:id="eq-state-distance-matrix">
        d_{\Xspace}(X,Y)
        =
        \frac{1}{2}\lVert X-Y\rVert_F^2.
      </md>
      The key distance
      <m>d^\star(X)=d_{\Xspace}(X,X^\star)</m> is both the number of cards not
      in their home zones and the minimum number of ordinary single-card moves
      required to reach the key.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-event-transitions">
    <title>Events as State Transitions and Marks</title>

    <p>
      A move is represented by <m>m=(j,i,h)</m>, where card <m>j</m> moves
      from zone <m>i</m> to zone <m>h</m>.  In the configuration vector, the
      move replaces coordinate <m>s(j)=i</m> with <m>s'(j)=h</m>.  In matrix
      form,
      <md xml:id="eq-move-update">
        X'
        =
        X+(e_h-e_i)f_j^{\mathsf T},
      </md>
      where <m>e_i</m> and <m>f_j</m> are standard basis vectors for zones
      and cards.  A move to zone <m>0</m> is a takeback.  A drop whose
      destination is the card's current zone is retained as a no-op event but
      does not change the state.
    </p>

    <p>
      A check leaves the current state unchanged but marks it as submitted to
      the grader and attaches the recorded result and feedback.  A reset is a
      distinguished jump back to <m>X_0</m>.  A page event does not change the
      board; it contributes evidence about access and attempt boundaries.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-trajectories">
    <title>Marked Timestamped Trajectories</title>

    <p>
      After processing and segmentation, an attempt is represented as
      <md xml:id="eq-event-trajectory">
        \Gamma
        =
        \bigl(
          (a_0,t_0,X_0),
          (a_1,t_1,X_1),
          \ldots,
          (a_n,t_n,X_n)
        \bigr),
      </md>
      where <m>a_k</m> is the processed action mark, <m>t_k</m> is the event
      time, and <m>X_k</m> is the state after the event.  The trajectory is
      marked because each time carries an event type and payload.  Two
      attempts can therefore visit the same states while differing in move
      order, checks, feedback exposure, resets, or elapsed time.
    </p>

    <p>
      Events are nested in attempts, attempts belong to student-item pairs,
      and student and item are crossed factors: students encounter multiple
      items, and items are encountered by multiple students.  Descriptive
      uncertainty and inferential models must account for dependence at these
      levels when the numbers of students and items support it.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-running-trajectory">
      <caption>
        Running example of an incremental log, reconstructed states, and check
        anchors.  Left: the shapes item as authored; dashes show the key
        <m>s^\star=(1,1,2,0)</m>, the circle is a decoy card, and Pentagon is a
        decoy slot.  Right, top to bottom: raw export rows (raw positional
        labels shown once, at <m>t_1</m>); the states reconstructed by replay;
        and the key distance <m>d^\star</m>.  The checks at <m>t_5</m> and
        <m>t_7</m> leave the state unchanged; their <c>Input</c> payloads are
        decoded independently of replay into checked configurations, and their
        agreement with the replayed states is the anchor comparison.
      </caption>
      <image source="figures/fig-running-trajectory.png" width="100%">
        <description>
          <p>
            A three-band diagram beside an item panel. The item panel shows a
            tray holding an equilateral triangle, a right triangle, a square, and
            a circle, above three slots labeled Triangle, Quadrilateral, and
            Pentagon; dashed outlines mark the key placement and Pentagon is
            empty in the key.  The top band shows seven raw log rows: four
            dragNdrop-drop moves, a check, a takeback move, and a second check.
            The middle band shows the replayed configuration vectors from
            (0,0,0,0) through (1,1,2,3) at the incorrect check to (1,1,2,0) at
            the correct check, with each check's independently decoded anchor
            configuration matching the replayed state.  The bottom band plots the
            key distance falling 3, 2, 1, 0, rising to 1 when the circle enters
            Pentagon, and returning to 0 after the takeback.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <!--
    RUNNING-EXAMPLE CHECKLIST
    1. Select an item that can be legally reproduced and understood without course context.
    2. Verify its source-order card and zone indices.
    3. Construct or select one short trace containing moves, an incorrect check,
        a revision, and a correct check.
    4. Confirm every displayed state against the pipeline output.
    5. Show the raw positional labels once, then use authored labels or indices.
    6. Annotate check anchors as independently decoded observations.
    7. Keep the figure legible in a single journal column or full-width page.
    -->
  </subsection>
</section>